---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Root-Finding Methods — CMNA"
  description="Compare bisection, Newton's method, and the secant method using the CMNA implementations."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Root finding · CMNA Laboratory</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Three roads to a root.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Bisection narrows a bracket. Newton follows a derivative. Secant estimates
        that derivative from recent function values. The answer may be the same; the
        path almost never is.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="comparison-intro">
        <article class="card">
          <span class="lab-badge">Robust</span>
          <h3>Bisection</h3>
          <p>
            Requires a sign-changing bracket. Convergence is steady and easy to
            bound because every successful step halves the interval.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="lab-badge">Fast when happy</span>
          <h3>Newton</h3>
          <p>
            Uses the derivative to jump toward the root. Local convergence can be
            excellent, but the initial value and derivative matter.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="lab-badge">Derivative-free</span>
          <h3>Secant</h3>
          <p>
            Replaces the exact derivative with a slope estimated from nearby points,
            trading some of Newton's speed for less information.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section lab-section">
    <div class="shell" data-root-compare>
      <div class="lab-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="section-kicker">Compare convergence</p>
          <h2 class="section-title">Same function. Different machinery.</h2>
          <p class="lede">
            The graph uses <code>log10 |f(x)|</code> so that several orders of
            magnitude of residual fit on one axis. Lower is better.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="lab-expression">
          <span>f(x)</span>
          <code data-expression></code>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="lab-controls">
        <label>
          Function
          <select data-preset>
            <option value="cubic">x³ − x − 2</option>
            <option value="fixed">cos(x) − x</option>
            <option value="sqrt2">x² − 2</option>
            <option value="book">CMNA cubic</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label>
          Tolerance
          <select data-tolerance>
            <option value="0.1">10⁻¹</option>
            <option value="0.01">10⁻²</option>
            <option value="0.001" selected>10⁻³</option>
            <option value="0.000001">10⁻⁶</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <button class="button lab-button" type="button" data-run>Run comparison</button>
      </div>

      <div class="compare-cards" data-cards></div>

      <div class="comparison-legend" aria-label="Chart legend">
        <span><i class="legend-azure"></i>Bisection</span>
        <span><i class="legend-murrey"></i>Newton</span>
        <span><i class="legend-gold"></i>Secant</span>
      </div>

      <div class="lab-plot-frame">
        <svg
          class="lab-plot"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Residual by iteration for bisection, Newton, and secant methods"
          data-plot
        ></svg>
      </div>

      <p class="lab-status" data-note></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-column">
      <article class="content">
        <h2>What the comparison is really showing</h2>
        <p>
          Iteration count is not the whole cost of a numerical method. Newton asks
          for a derivative evaluation at every step. Bisection asks only for function
          values but needs a valid bracket. The secant method avoids an explicit
          derivative, but the estimated slope can become poor or collapse.
        </p>
        <p>
          The laboratory therefore treats the graph as a way to inspect behavior, not
          a scoreboard declaring one method universally superior. Numerical analysis
          is mostly the art of knowing which assumptions you can afford.
        </p>
      </article>

      <aside class="callout">
        <strong>Implementation note</strong>
        <p>
          Newton and secant here follow the control flow of the current
          <code>cmna</code> package implementations, including their initial
          perturbation by <code>10 * tol</code>. Bisection uses the bracket listed
          with each example.
        </p>
        <a href="https://github.com/k3jph/cmna-pkg/tree/main/R">Inspect the package source →</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline type="module" src="/js/root-compare.js"></script>
</BaseLayout>
